1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,129
Sworn is for Tempo Zones: private ledgers on Tempo.

2
00:00:04,179 --> 00:00:09,236
Every screen in this demo is real: a real proof job,

3
00:00:09,286 --> 00:00:12,950
real testnet transactions, and a real test run.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,950
In Sworn's local console,

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,450
the operator starts a proof job for a Zone batch with one withdrawal and two user transactions.

6
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:27,950
Tempo's own Zone code runs inside a zero-knowledge VM.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,950
This is the real job; we cut from its start to its verified result.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:44,080
A contract on Moderato checked that proof against the inputs Tempo's verifier interface expects.

9
00:00:44,130 --> 00:00:48,646
Here is the transaction on Tempo's explorer.

10
00:00:48,696 --> 00:00:59,080
Back on the page, live: the real batch verifies, and with one field changed it reverts.

11
00:00:59,130 --> 00:01:04,950
Moderato's current prototype verifier accepts even a malformed batch.

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:08,518
Now our own Zone on Moderato.

13
00:01:08,568 --> 00:01:14,464
Its portal called this verifier in each of three batches,

14
00:01:14,514 --> 00:01:26,950
and only after the last proof passed did our sequencer pay the withdrawal: zero point five pathUSD, here on the explorer.

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:36,080
Then our sequencer, with a valid signature, submitted a forged batch replaying a real proof.

16
00:01:36,130 --> 00:01:40,950
The explorer's trace shows the verifier rejecting it.

17
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,563
Anyone can re-check the three live proofs.

18
00:01:44,613 --> 00:01:49,724
This script takes each verify call from its transaction trace;

19
00:01:49,774 --> 00:01:56,950
the tests run each proof against the real SP1 verifier and the deployed bytecode.

20
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,664
To be clear: the proof job used a batch from Tempo's tests,

21
00:02:02,714 --> 00:02:07,902
and the Zone that paid is our own, run by us.

22
00:02:07,952 --> 00:02:09,331
No customers yet.

23
00:02:09,381 --> 00:02:16,950
Next, a Zone business supplies a batch, and its reviewer decides if it's worth paying for.
